Phase 4: Prepared graphics pipelines and executable compatibility
  Dependencies: Phases 1–3 including Subphases 2.1–2.2, executable revision and same-live-context lifetime policy.
  Outcome: validated GraphicsPipeline realizations usable by submission without per-draw reflection.

  ☐ Compose existing shader readiness, GpuBindingContract, GpuPreparedBindings and generated inputs.
    ☐ Validate active stages, vertex attributes/scalar interpretation/components/divisors, fragment output
      locations/scalar classes, resource layout, static state, target signature and capabilities.
    ☐ Add missing interface validation at preparation time; do not build parallel reflection/binding systems.
    ☐ Derive built-in ClipDistance outputs and array extent for the final vertex-producing stage of
      each actual compiled variant through the existing compiler/interface ownership path. Current
      GpuProgramInterface/GpuPreparedBindings reflect resources, not these built-in outputs.
      Resolve specialization-dependent extents; handle optimized binaries and absent debug names.
      Do not reintroduce authored output masks.
    ☐ Validate enabled clip-distance bits against verified compiled outputs; reject missing or
      unverifiable outputs before publishing a usable pipeline. Reflection does not prove writes
      on every control-flow path; retain the shader author's defined-output obligation.
    ☐ Add compiled-shader/preparation fixtures for absent/present outputs, out-of-range enabled bits,
      variant-dependent outputs and final vertex/geometry/tessellation-evaluation stage selection.
      Cover Debug/Release reflection, failed publication and unchanged-input metadata reuse.
  ☐ Publish prepared objects only after successful validation.
    ☐ Retain needed dependency references without acquiring ownership of externally owned shader resources.
    ☐ Reject retired dependencies and obsolete executable revisions; confine use to the owning renderer lifetime.
    ☐ Invalidate on reload; revalidate incompatible changes before publishing replacements.
    ☐ Preserve existing deferred deletion; do not invent ResourceId for a managed aggregate.
    ☐ Use explicit prepared objects; defer registry/interning and bound future cache requirements in docs.
  ☐ Delegate tests for interface/format/sample mismatch, unsupported stages/settings, failed preparation,
    compatible/incompatible reload, explicit disposal and renderer teardown within the same live context.
  ☐ Completion gate: failures cannot expose a partially usable pipeline; no steady-state interface queries.

